  "title": "Type 2 diabetes predicted effector genes (Atla G et al. 2022)",
  "bodyHtml": "<p>This table displays predicted effector genes for type 2 diabetes, from the study:</p><p>Genetic regulation of RNA splicing in human pancreatic islets.<br>Atla G, <em>et al.</em><br>Genome Biol . 2022 Sep 15;23(1):196. doi: 10.1186/s13059-022-02757-0.<br><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36109769/\" target=\"_blank\">PMID: 36109769</a></p><p>The authors mapped quantitative trait loci (QTLs) for mRNA levels (eQTLs) and splice junction usage (sQTLs) in 399 human pancreatic islets. To identify candidate effector genes, the authors performed a transcriptome-wide association study (TWAS) using the FUSION algorithm (<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26854917/\" target=\"_blank\">Gusev <em>et al</em>., 2016</a>) with T2D (adjusted for BMI) associations from the <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30297969/\" target=\"_blank\">Mahajan <em>et al</em>., 2018</a> study. For this purpose, expression and splicing junction usage weights were computed using RNA-seq and imputed genotypes generated in the study.</p><p>To complement TWAS results, the authors conducted colocalization analyses to test whether eQTLs and sQTLs were colocalized with GWAS associations for T2D adjusted for BMI. Genes identified from TWAS analyses whose eQTLs or sQTLs colocalized with a GWAS signal with posterior probability ≥ 0.6, shown in the table below, represent candidate effector genes for T2D.</p><p>Explore other predicted effector gene lists from this study:&nbsp; <a href=\"/research.html?pageid=Atla_FI_566\" target=\"_blank\">Fasting insulin</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp; <a href=\"/research.html?pageid=Atla_FG_530\" target=\"_blank\">Fasting glucose</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp; <a href=\"/research.html?pageid=Atla_T1D_568\" target=\"_blank\">Type 1 diabetes</a></p>",
